  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Roupp gave them six competent innings. Sam Hentges and Keaton Winn couldn't protect a lead in the seventh.",
    "what_this_means": "They held the lead until they didn't. Roupp's line was clean. Four hits, two runs, seven strikeouts over six. Exactly what you ask a starter to deliver on the road. Casey Schmitt hit one to keep them alive. Jung Hoo Lee drove in a run. Two conversion moments in seven RISP situations. That's the margin they're working with, and it collapsed when it mattered most. Hentges gave up two in a third of an inning. Winn surrendered one more. The lead turned over in a span that felt inevitable in retrospect. The kind of inning that makes you wonder if it was ever really safe to begin with.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like compressed into one afternoon. Good pitching meets bad relief. Seven runners left on base. Two runs scored from them. They were never chasing, but they were never comfortable either. That's the pattern again. They build something, then hand it back. The bullpen didn't hold the door. Everything after the sixth was leaving early.",
